WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.789
Embedded devices receive input from more
places than developers sometimes expect.

2
00:00:04.869 --> 00:00:08.313
Data may come from sensors, field buses,
serial ports, radio links,

3
00:00:08.393 --> 00:00:11.534
network packets, files, local commands,
firmware metadata,

4
00:00:11.614 --> 00:00:15.239
configuration storage, manufacturing
tools, or service procedures.

5
00:00:15.319 --> 00:00:17.736
Each input path can influence device
behavior.

6
00:00:17.816 --> 00:00:21.863
External input should be treated as
untrusted until it is validated against the

7
00:00:21.943 --> 00:00:24.299
expected format and the current device
state.

8
00:00:24.379 --> 00:00:26.433
Local does not always mean trustworthy.

9
00:00:26.513 --> 00:00:29.896
A peripheral can report stale data, a bus
message can be malformed,

10
00:00:29.976 --> 00:00:33.964
configuration can be inconsistent, and a
command can arrive at the wrong time.

11
00:00:34.044 --> 00:00:37.427
Parsers should check lengths, counts,
offsets, versions, flags,

12
00:00:37.507 --> 00:00:41.434
checksums where the format uses them, and
message order before using values.

13
00:00:41.514 --> 00:00:44.353
A length field should be checked against
the input size,

14
00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:47.333
destination capacity, product limit, and
parser state.

15
00:00:47.413 --> 00:00:51.883
Validation should happen before allocating
memory or writing into output structures.

16
00:00:51.963 --> 00:00:55.286
State machines help prevent commands from
running before setup,

17
00:00:55.366 --> 00:00:59.293
authorization, configuration, or
prerequisite validation has completed.

18
00:00:59.373 --> 00:01:01.789
A state machine does not need to be
complicated.

19
00:01:01.869 --> 00:01:05.011
The important part is that allowed
transitions are explicit,

20
00:01:05.091 --> 00:01:07.266
rejected transitions are handled safely,

21
00:01:07.346 --> 00:01:10.668
and partially processed input cannot
masquerade as valid state.

22
00:01:10.748 --> 00:01:15.098
Invalid or malformed input should fail
safely without corrupting state or consuming

23
00:01:15.178 --> 00:01:16.326
unbounded resources.

24
00:01:16.406 --> 00:01:19.306
The device might reject the command, reset
parser state,

25
00:01:19.386 --> 00:01:22.104
return a bounded error, record a compact
diagnostic,

26
00:01:22.184 --> 00:01:24.782
or continue with the previous safe
configuration.

27
00:01:24.862 --> 00:01:26.735
The behavior should be predictable.

28
00:01:26.815 --> 00:01:29.896
Validation logic should be simple enough
to test and review.

29
00:01:29.976 --> 00:01:33.963
Boundary tests should cover minimum and
maximum sizes, just-outside limits,

30
00:01:34.043 --> 00:01:37.305
missing fields, bad versions, unexpected
order, full buffers,

31
00:01:37.385 --> 00:01:38.956
and recovery after rejection.

32
00:01:39.036 --> 00:01:41.453
If reviewers cannot explain the parser
states,

33
00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:43.768
the parser is probably too hard to
maintain.

34
00:01:43.848 --> 00:01:46.748
The most useful validation code is boring
in the best way.

35
00:01:46.828 --> 00:01:50.875
It states what is accepted, rejects
everything else, keeps memory use bounded,

36
00:01:50.955 --> 00:01:54.580
and leaves the device in a known state
when input is not suitable for use.
